  "descriptions": [
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs",
      "text": "unverified external call. Lost: ~2M"
    },
    {
      "source": "REKT",
      "text": "March 8th, $2.1M was stolen in multiple attacks on the ETH-based DEX shortly after a contract upgrade to their DEX aggregation contract."
    },
    {
      "source": "DeFiHackLabs explorer",
      "text": "Beacuse of the Arbitrary call, the attacker were able to access and drain value from the accounts of users who had created approvals for the contract. Attack Tx: https://app.blocksec.com/explorer/tx/eth/0x923d1d63a1165ebd3521516f6d22d015f2e1b4b22d5dc954152b6c089c765fcd Analysis: https://twitter.com/SlowMist_Team/status/1766311510362734824"
    },
    {
      "source": "SlowMist",
      "text": "The Unizen defi platform lost around $2.1 million in the Tether stablecoin in an attack that took advantage of a vulnerability an external call from the project smart contract. On March 12th, Unizen's CTO Martin Granström tweeted that they had recovered $185,000 worth of stolen funds from four hackers."
    },
    {
      "source": "RevertIQ",
      "text": "Unizen's DEX aggregator proxy contract on Ethereum Mainnet was exploited on 8 March 2024 due to an unsafe external call vulnerability introduced in a contract upgrade, resulting in losses of about $2.1 million. The attacker drained user-approved USDT, quickly swapping to DAI and ETH. Most affected users (those with losses under $750,000) were reimbursed shortly after the incident. The exploit arose from an unsafe external call vulnerability introduced during a smart contract upgrade to Unizen's DEX aggregation proxy, allowing attackers to invoke arbitrary contract methods and withdraw funds from users who had previously granted token approvals."
    }
  ],
